WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.209
Queues distribute pending work across consumers and
smooth a temporary mismatch between arrival and

00:00:06.209 --> 00:00:07.275
processing rates.

00:00:07.275 --> 00:00:13.483
Producers and consumers can scale independently, but
the broker has its own capacity and durability

00:00:13.483 --> 00:00:13.922
limits.

00:00:13.922 --> 00:00:17.811
An in-process queue is simple but may vanish with
the process.

00:00:17.811 --> 00:00:23.894
A separate broker or replicated service provides
different persistence and recovery options, with

00:00:23.894 --> 00:00:26.716
additional operational cost and coordination.

00:00:26.716 --> 00:00:33.121
A bounded queue must eventually reject, delay or
otherwise regulate producers when it cannot safely

00:00:33.121 --> 00:00:34.220
accept more work.

00:00:34.220 --> 00:00:39.266
This backpressure is a protective control rather
than merely an error to hide.

00:00:39.266 --> 00:00:45.348
Monitor oldest-message age as well as depth, because
a small queue of expensive jobs can still

00:00:45.348 --> 00:00:46.771
violate latency goals.

00:00:46.771 --> 00:00:52.464
Retry policies should use limits and backoff instead
of continuously recycling failures.
